1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,170
Every second, software makes
thousands of small decisions.

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:09,820
Which team should get this ticket?
Is this prompt a jailbreak?

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,430
How urgent is this email?

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,580
None of these are open questions.

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,400
Each has a small, known set of answers,

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,620
and that shape of problem
deserves its own kind of model.

7
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:23,830
Meet System 1 decision engines:

8
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:27,570
models that answer typed questions
in a single forward pass,

9
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:30,190
with a probability for every allowed answer.

10
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:35,630
Our reference implementation is Laya, an
open-source, multilingual decision engine.

11
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:41,073
Today, the default is to
ask a large language model.

12
00:00:41,523 --> 00:00:44,743
It writes the answer one token at a time.

13
00:00:44,743 --> 00:00:48,983
Then your code parses the text,
checks the label is one you allowed,

14
00:00:48,983 --> 00:00:50,553
and retries when it isn't.

15
00:00:51,003 --> 00:00:54,763
A full generation, a
parser and a retry path,

16
00:00:54,763 --> 00:00:57,633
for a decision with only
three possible answers.

17
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:03,573
The name comes from Daniel
Kahneman's Thinking, Fast and Slow,

18
00:01:03,573 --> 00:01:05,383
which describes two modes of thought.

19
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:09,673
System 1 is fast, automatic and cheap:

20
00:01:09,673 --> 00:01:12,933
recognizing a face, sensing
that a sentence is hostile.

21
00:01:13,383 --> 00:01:18,163
System 2 is slow, deliberate and expensive:

22
00:01:18,163 --> 00:01:20,713
long multiplication, checking an argument.

23
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:24,043
Software has the same split.

24
00:01:24,043 --> 00:01:27,543
Language models made System 2
cheap enough to use everywhere,

25
00:01:27,543 --> 00:01:31,963
but most production decisions
are still System 1 work:

26
00:01:31,963 --> 00:01:37,183
known options, on every request, where
speed and consistency matter most.

27
00:01:37,633 --> 00:01:40,253
So the fast system should answer first,

28
00:01:40,253 --> 00:01:43,573
and its confidence should decide
when to call in the slow one.

29
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,830
Here's the core difference.

30
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,640
An autoregressive model generates
its answer token by token.

31
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:57,000
Each token is another sequential forward
pass, and the output can be malformed,

32
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,690
or a label you never offered.

33
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:04,360
A decision engine never writes the label.
The options are part of its input.

34
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,880
One forward pass scores
every option side by side,

35
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:13,100
and a softmax turns those scores into a
distribution over exactly those options.

36
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:16,350
So the answer is always valid.

37
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:20,970
Every option gets a probability, so
you see how close the runner-up was.

38
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:23,390
And the cost doesn't depend on the answer.

39
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,410
Every decision is expressed
with three question types.

40
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:34,260
Choice picks one label from several, like
which department should handle a request.

41
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:38,030
The label descriptions are what the
model actually matches against.

42
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:43,040
Score places the input on an
ordered scale, like urgency,

43
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,360
and returns the expected level:

44
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:47,800
a probability-weighted
average of the levels.

45
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:53,690
And noul returns the probability that
a statement is true. Is this phishing?

46
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:55,340
Does the user threaten to cancel?

47
00:02:55,790 --> 00:03:00,680
Several questions about the same input are
answered together, in one batched call.

48
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:06,310
Inside, each question
becomes one row of tokens.

49
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:09,560
A header with the question
type and instructions.

50
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:10,920
Then every option,

51
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:14,800
each preceded by a mask token that
marks where its score will be read.

52
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,230
Then the input itself.

53
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:22,200
The encoder is bidirectional, so each
marker sees its option, the question,

54
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,440
the rival options and
the input, all at once.

55
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:29,290
But the options share
one fixed token budget.

56
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:33,370
With seventy-seven options, each
label keeps about three tokens,

57
00:03:33,370 --> 00:03:36,550
and similar labels collapse
into the same text.

58
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:40,620
The rule of thumb: keep choice
questions under about twenty options.

59
00:03:42,233 --> 00:03:45,063
Now follow that row through the model.

60
00:03:45,513 --> 00:03:50,613
A pretrained encoder, ModernBERT large
for English, reads the whole row.

61
00:03:50,613 --> 00:03:53,193
A type embedding says
which primitive this is,

62
00:03:53,193 --> 00:03:56,773
and a small two-layer decision
head refines every position.

63
00:03:57,223 --> 00:04:00,383
The model gathers the hidden
vector at each marker,

64
00:04:00,383 --> 00:04:04,183
and a shared scorer turns each
one into a single number:

65
00:04:04,183 --> 00:04:05,553
one logit per option.

66
00:04:06,003 --> 00:04:09,903
Because options are inputs,
not fixed output units,

67
00:04:09,903 --> 00:04:13,303
the same weights can score
two options or twenty,

68
00:04:13,303 --> 00:04:15,713
even labels the model has never seen.

69
00:04:16,163 --> 00:04:18,003
On a Tesla T4,

70
00:04:18,003 --> 00:04:22,643
one question takes about thirty-three
milliseconds on the multilingual checkpoint.

71
00:04:22,643 --> 00:04:26,523
Ten questions take seventy-two:
about seven milliseconds each.

72
00:04:28,133 --> 00:04:33,993
Decoding is a few lines: divide the logits
by a temperature, then apply a softmax.

73
00:04:34,443 --> 00:04:37,663
Temperature never changes which option wins.

74
00:04:37,663 --> 00:04:40,643
It only changes how
confident the model sounds.

75
00:04:41,093 --> 00:04:46,113
And of the two confidence numbers the
engine reports, gate on answer confidence,

76
00:04:46,113 --> 00:04:48,193
the probability of the chosen answer.

77
00:04:48,193 --> 00:04:50,463
That's the number calibration is fitted on.

78
00:04:52,067 --> 00:04:57,027
For those probabilities to mean
anything, honesty has to be trained in.

79
00:04:57,477 --> 00:05:00,797
The tool is a strictly proper scoring rule:

80
00:05:00,797 --> 00:05:05,947
a reward that is maximized, on average,
only by reporting what you truly believe.

81
00:05:06,397 --> 00:05:09,397
If the truth is seventy percent, the log,

82
00:05:09,397 --> 00:05:13,677
spherical and Brier scores
all peak at exactly seventy.

83
00:05:13,677 --> 00:05:19,437
A linear reward keeps climbing towards
certainty: it pays for overconfidence.

84
00:05:19,437 --> 00:05:23,597
And plain accuracy can't tell
fifty-one percent from ninety-nine.

85
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:30,000
Laya's training method builds
on this. It's called RLCD:

86
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,860
Reinforcement Learning
for Calibrated Decisions.

87
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:34,490
For each item,

88
00:05:34,490 --> 00:05:39,430
it adds noise to the logits to sample a
small group of candidate distributions,

89
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:43,730
rewards each with a proper scoring rule,
and compares it with its group's average.

90
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:47,420
The logits move towards
the above-average samples,

91
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:52,100
while a soft cross-entropy term pulls
towards the teacher's full distribution,

92
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:53,260
uncertainty included.

93
00:05:53,710 --> 00:05:57,130
Fine-tuning is where the
accuracy comes from.

94
00:05:57,130 --> 00:06:01,970
On the typed-decisions benchmark, the
base model scores zero point three six.

95
00:06:01,970 --> 00:06:05,070
Fine-tuned, it reaches
zero point seven seven,

96
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:07,240
above the teacher's own self-agreement.

97
00:06:08,867 --> 00:06:13,087
A model is calibrated when its
confidence means what it says:

98
00:06:13,087 --> 00:06:16,867
of the answers given at eighty percent,
about eighty percent are right.

99
00:06:17,317 --> 00:06:20,157
The shipped checkpoints are over-confident.

100
00:06:20,157 --> 00:06:23,877
The fix is temperature scaling,
fitted on held-out data.

101
00:06:23,877 --> 00:06:25,397
It never changes an answer,

102
00:06:25,397 --> 00:06:30,757
and it cuts calibration error from zero
point four seven to zero point zero eight.

103
00:06:31,207 --> 00:06:35,387
But calibration can't rescue an
input the model cannot read.

104
00:06:35,387 --> 00:06:39,227
On Khmer, the English checkpoint
scored zero accuracy,

105
00:06:39,227 --> 00:06:41,107
at ninety-five percent confidence.

106
00:06:42,733 --> 00:06:46,253
That's why Laya ships three
checkpoints and a router.

107
00:06:46,703 --> 00:06:51,383
One for English, a faster multilingual
one for over a hundred languages,

108
00:06:51,383 --> 00:06:54,843
and one fine-tuned for
four specific workflows.

109
00:06:55,293 --> 00:06:57,393
Before any forward pass,

110
00:06:57,393 --> 00:07:01,493
the router detects script and
language in well under a millisecond,

111
00:07:01,493 --> 00:07:04,893
and sends the text to a
checkpoint that can read it.

112
00:07:04,893 --> 00:07:08,993
Across fifty-one languages,
usable coverage nearly doubles:

113
00:07:08,993 --> 00:07:11,313
from twenty-three to forty-five.

114
00:07:12,933 --> 00:07:17,473
In production, a decision engine
returns evidence, not permission.

115
00:07:17,923 --> 00:07:20,643
The core pattern is confidence gating:

116
00:07:20,643 --> 00:07:25,583
above a threshold, act automatically;
below it, escalate to a human, a rule,

117
00:07:25,583 --> 00:07:27,223
or a larger model.

118
00:07:27,223 --> 00:07:31,953
The threshold is a policy, chosen from
measured accuracy and the cost of mistakes.

119
00:07:32,403 --> 00:07:34,463
Roll out in stages:

120
00:07:34,463 --> 00:07:39,563
shadow, compare, choose a policy, then
promote a bounded slice of traffic,

121
00:07:39,563 --> 00:07:40,703
with a rollback plan.

122
00:07:42,333 --> 00:07:45,093
This matters most inside agents.

123
00:07:45,543 --> 00:07:51,103
An agent loop is full of small,
typed decisions. Is this input safe?

124
00:07:51,103 --> 00:07:56,103
Which tool comes next? Is the task done?
Is this output good enough to send?

125
00:07:56,553 --> 00:07:59,153
System 1 takes those in milliseconds,

126
00:07:59,153 --> 00:08:02,223
and hands only the uncertain
cases to System 2.

127
00:08:02,673 --> 00:08:08,093
In one browser agent, fine-tuning lifted
the right-element choice from ten percent,

128
00:08:08,093 --> 00:08:10,953
which is chance, to nearly two thirds,

129
00:08:10,953 --> 00:08:14,003
with each step decided in
about twenty milliseconds.

130
00:08:15,633 --> 00:08:19,623
It isn't magic, and the
limits deserve plain words.

131
00:08:20,073 --> 00:08:22,713
A decision engine is not a reasoner:

132
00:08:22,713 --> 00:08:26,753
it can't explain itself
or answer open questions.

133
00:08:26,753 --> 00:08:31,413
Zero-shot, the base checkpoints are
near chance on specialized workflows,

134
00:08:31,413 --> 00:08:36,313
they ship over-confident, and long option
lists or other languages need care.

135
00:08:36,763 --> 00:08:41,963
So evaluate on your own traffic,
fine-tune on your own decisions,

136
00:08:41,963 --> 00:08:46,793
fit temperatures on held-out data,
and gate every change in CI.

137
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:53,670
Let System 1 answer first: in one
pass, with honest probabilities.

138
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:58,320
And let calibrated confidence decide
when it's time to think slow.

139
00:08:58,770 --> 00:09:01,690
That's System 1 decision engines.

140
00:09:01,690 --> 00:09:04,720
Explore all eighteen chapters
in the interactive tutorial.
